cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this repository, please cite it as below -- and carry the scope note in the abstract
  with any number you quote from it.
title: >-
  psr-bilevel-eme-boundary: a reproduction of a public bi-level adiabatic polarization
  splitter-rotator (SOI 220 nm + 90 nm partial etch), and the boundary of a mode-expansion
  insertion loss -- port basis, discretisation and solver defaults
abstract: >-
  A reproduction of the public BilevelPSR design on an SOI 220 nm + 90 nm partial-etch platform,
  verified in two independent segments by two different numerical methods: the taper segment by
  3D FDTD (TM0 -> TE1 = 99.84%) and the coupler segment by 3D EME (TE1 -> lower-arm TE0 = 99.233%,
  passing a 4 -> 6 port-mode convergence check, delta = -0.29%).


  The boundary is stated as a property of the numerical object, not of the device: a minimal S-bend,
  re-read on a second solver (EME, varFDTD and an exact bent-waveguide FDE solve, recorded in
  second_solver/), reads 0.055 / 0.765 / lossless for one nominal structure, and each numerical
  choice moves the reading once its feature is amplified.


  SCOPE -- this is part of the citation, not a disclaimer added afterwards. Only the two
  port-mode-basis conversions above may be quoted. Device-level absolute insertion loss and return
  loss are NOT certified here: in this structure the EME port-power column sums stay close to 1
  even after adding port modes, so no radiation-loss channel appears inside the port-mode basis,
  and that figure would require an all-device 3D FDTD run or a measurement. The output section
  (S-bend plus the straight section, x = 405..537.8 um) was not covered. The falsification is
  scoped to this structure, this length scale and the 4 -> 6 mode comparison; it is not a general
  claim about eigenmode expansion.
